course: dictionary
lesson: 6017
title: Medieval: a word with two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Medieval is a word about a long time in history. It means from or about the Middle Ages, the long period in Europe after Roman times and before the modern age. «Spices were dear in medieval Europe but not usually worth as much as gold.» The words medieval Europe tell you the time, so you can picture castles, knights and kings.
explain: The word has a second, informal meaning too. When someone says a rule is medieval, they mean it is very old-fashioned or cruel. To choose the right meaning, read the context. A history book is talking about the time, but a complaint about a school rule is probably using the informal meaning.
explain: Try the clues. «In medieval Europe some people linked black cats with witches, but in Britain and Japan black cats can mean good luck.» Medieval points to a past time, and the words "in Britain and Japan" show that the belief was not shared everywhere.
fact: Medieval means from or about the Middle Ages.
fact: The Middle Ages were the long period in European history after Roman times and before the modern age.
fact: Informally, medieval can mean very old-fashioned or cruel.
fact: «Medieval people washed their hands and faces every day, and many towns had public bathhouses.»
fact: Common phrases include medieval castle, medieval times and medieval knight.
exercise: «Spices were dear in medieval Europe.» In this sentence, what does medieval mean? | Very old-fashioned | *From or about the Middle Ages | Near the sea | Made of metal
exercise: Mia says her teacher's rule is medieval, because it is cruel. Which meaning is she using? | It means the same as modern | The meaning of a castle with towers | The history meaning: from the Middle Ages | *The informal meaning: very old-fashioned or cruel
exercise: «The medieval castle stood on the hill.» Find and copy the word that tells you the time period. | hill | stood | *medieval | castle
test: «In medieval Europe a sack of pepper could be worth a fortune.» What does medieval tell us? | It means the pepper was brand new | *It places the story in the Middle Ages | It means the pepper was very small | It means the pepper was cruel
test: «Some medieval ports have silted up, as Bruges did when trade moved to Antwerp.» What does medieval mean in this sentence? | Fast-moving | Very old-fashioned, like a silly rule | *From or about the Middle Ages | Very cruel
exam: Which sentence uses medieval in the informal sense? | «Medieval knights wore armour in stories.» | «Medieval times are part of European history.» | «The medieval port is on the coast.» | *«Making us stand silent for an hour at lunch is medieval.»
